Hi all,

forwarding the port authority notice from 14 March (it landed in the general inbox and sat there 8 days, sorry, that's on me). Short version for those who will not read all of it:

1) we have to put every fixed refrigeration unit with 3 kg or more of fluorinated refrigerant on the port environmental register by 30 June 2024. Gunnar - I think this means our yard gensets don't count but the reefer units on pedestals do, and every one of the units has to be listed individually with serial number, refrigerant type and charge. Can you take it? I'd like a draft by 31 May so Sofia can look at the declaration wording.

2) leak checks are tied to charge size (table in the notice, point 3). Ours are all below 30 kg so it is once a year for everything, but they want the checker to hold a certificate and the logbook to be kept at the site, not at HQ. Please find out if Thermaglide's service engineers hold the right certificate - I suspect they do but I'd rather have it in writing.

3) the night noise limit is the one that will hurt. 50 dB(A) at the boundary between 22:00 and 06:00. The port will measure during a campaign from 8 to 12 April, not announced in advance (but they told us in the notice, so, you know). Jarek: pad D is the problem, we know that from the neighbours' letter last year. Can we switch off the pad D idle reefers' fan speed boost at night? Gunnar says maybe, ask him.

4) any spill over 20 litres or any refrigerant release of 2 kg or more must be phoned to port control within 4 hours. I will put the number on the dispatch desk laminated card. (+48 58 555 0162, 24 h.)

Deadline for the acknowledgement with the name of our environmental contact is 12 April. I propose it is me for now, with Gunnar as deputy. Meeting on this Wednesday 27 March, 10:00, small meeting room at HQ, 30 min. Kamil, you are cc only because of the pedestal inspection certificates, which I think you hold.

Fines per breach are 2,000 to 25,000 PLN, see point 7. I don't want to find out where in that range we would land.

B.

